From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:36:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Stop paying per world per tick for worlds nobody is in

Two allocations in the tick loop that scale with world count rather than
with anything being used. On a prison server running a world per private
mine, world count is the number that grows.

Time updates (MinecraftServer). A PacketPlayOutUpdateTime was built, and
doDaylightCycle looked up by string key, once per world per tick - before
checking whether the world had any players. The loop that follows
iterates world.players, so an empty world produced nothing either way;
this stops paying for the nothing. The shared packet is also built on
first use, so a tick whose frequency check admits nobody builds none.

Entity tracker (EntityTracker.updatePlayers). Allocated a fresh ArrayList
every tick for every world, to hold players whose position moved far
enough to need a rescan - overwhelmingly empty on a server with many
small worlds. It is now a reused buffer, cleared before use and again
after, so player references are not held between ticks.

track() itself is still called for every world. It keeps position deltas
and tick counters the tracker relies on, so skipping it for an empty
world would freeze state rather than save work - the allocation was the
waste, not the call.

At 200 worlds this is 400 fewer objects per tick, or 8000 a second. The
effect on a three-world test server is below the noise floor; the place
to read it is GC collections in /vs perf on a server with the world count
that made it worth doing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/EntityTracker.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/EntityTracker.java
index cb72b36725927272fddfdad929b268a7b06140ac..089dda962594597594920c2950ca9c5db435e404 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/EntityTracker.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/EntityTracker.java
@@ -167,8 +167,15 @@ public class EntityTracker {
 
     }
 
+    // VoidSoulSpigot - reused across ticks. This was allocated fresh every tick for every
+    // world, and only ever holds players whose position moved enough to need a rescan - so
+    // on a server with many small worlds it was overwhelmingly an empty list allocated and
+    // thrown away. Main thread only, and cleared before use.
+    private final ArrayList<EntityPlayer> voidsoulMovedPlayers = new ArrayList<EntityPlayer>();
+
     public void updatePlayers() {
-        ArrayList arraylist = Lists.newArrayList();
+        ArrayList arraylist = this.voidsoulMovedPlayers; // VoidSoulSpigot - was Lists.newArrayList()
+        arraylist.clear(); // VoidSoulSpigot
         Iterator iterator = this.c.iterator();
 
         while (iterator.hasNext()) {
@@ -193,6 +200,7 @@ public class EntityTracker {
             }
         }
 
+        arraylist.clear(); // VoidSoulSpigot - do not hold player references between ticks
     }
 
     public void a(EntityPlayer entityplayer) {
diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer.java
index 5dd502a5e09b8e702cd644e81316ecdf88139066..af76c32d7c678c599b8ad936cb6d815d3f512dcb 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/MinecraftServer.java
@@ -887,18 +887,43 @@ public abstract class MinecraftServer implements Runnable, ICommandListener, IAs
         // Send time updates to everyone, it will get the right time from the world the player is in.
         // PandaSpigot start - optimize time updates
         for (final WorldServer world : this.worlds) {
+            // VoidSoulSpigot start - do none of this for a world nobody is in.
+            //
+            // The packet below was built, and the game rule looked up by string key, once
+            // per world per tick regardless of whether anyone would receive it. A prison
+            // server with two hundred private mines paid two hundred allocations and two
+            // hundred map lookups every tick for packets that were discarded. The loop
+            // that follows iterates world.players, so an empty world produced nothing
+            // either way - this only stops paying for the nothing.
+            if (world.players.isEmpty()) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            // VoidSoulSpigot end
             final boolean doDaylight = world.getGameRules().getBoolean("doDaylightCycle");
             final long dayTime = world.getDayTime();
             long worldTime = world.getTime();
-            final PacketPlayOutUpdateTime worldPacket = new PacketPlayOutUpdateTime(worldTime, dayTime, doDaylight);
+            PacketPlayOutUpdateTime worldPacket = null; // VoidSoulSpigot - built on first use
             for (EntityHuman entityhuman : world.players) {
                 if (!(entityhuman instanceof EntityPlayer) || (this.ticks + entityhuman.getId()) % world.pandaSpigotConfig.timeUpdateFrequency != 0) {
                     continue;
                 }
                 EntityPlayer entityplayer = (EntityPlayer) entityhuman;
                 long playerTime = entityplayer.getPlayerTime();
-                PacketPlayOutUpdateTime packet = (playerTime == dayTime) ? worldPacket :
-                        new PacketPlayOutUpdateTime(worldTime, playerTime, doDaylight);
+                // VoidSoulSpigot - the shared packet is built the first time a player is
+                // actually due one. Players on their own time never build it at all, and
+                // the frequency check above means most ticks reach here for nobody.
+                PacketPlayOutUpdateTime packet;
+
+                if (playerTime == dayTime) {
+                    if (worldPacket == null) {
+                        worldPacket = new PacketPlayOutUpdateTime(worldTime, dayTime, doDaylight);
+                    }
+
+                    packet = worldPacket;
+                } else {
+                    packet = new PacketPlayOutUpdateTime(worldTime, playerTime, doDaylight);
+                }
+
                 entityplayer.playerConnection.sendPacket(packet); // Add support for per player time
             }
         }
